t of known Brazilian club names (from match data + knowledge) and match after normalization. E.g. Grêmio, Santos, Fluminense, Botafogo, Cruzeiro, Atlético Mineiro, Internacional, Bahia, Sport Club do Recife, Atlético Paranaense (or Athletico), Chapecoense, Ceará Sporting Club, Vitória, Paraná, América FC (Minas Gerais), Avaí, Goiás, Coritiba, Fortaleza, CSA, Atlético Goianiense, etc. Let me print all clubs that contain any player with Brazilian nationality and have 'FC'/'Sport'/'Club' patterns... no, let me just print all clubs from FIFA with names that normalize-match team names present in the match datasets (intersection of club names and match team names). That gives Brazilian clubs automatically.\n\nLet me now gather unique team names from each match file to design the alias table. Then design the package.\n\nPlan the file layout:\n\n```\nserver.py                      # MCP entry point (FastMCP, stdio)\nbrazilian_soccer/\n  __init__.py\n  normalize.py                 # team name normalization, canonical aliases\n  dates.py                     # multi-format date parsing\n  models.py                    # Match, Player dataclasses\n  loader.py                    # SoccerData: loads all 6 CSVs, unified\n  queries.py                   # query layer: matches, team stats, h2h, standings, players, agg stats, derbies\ntests/\n  conftest.py                  # session fixture loading data\n  test_match_queries.py        # BDD GWT\n  test_team_queries.py\n  test_player_queries.py\n  test_competition_queries.py\n  test_statistical_analysis.py\n  test_normalize_dates.py      # data quality: name variations, date formats, UTF-8\n  test_mcp_server.py           # MCP tool smoke test via in-memory Client\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md (update)\n```\n\nContext block comment at the start of every code file (per prompts.txt sample: \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\"). Though that's from prompts.txt which says to ignore... The user said implement TASK.md. TASK.md doesn't require context block comments. But it doesn't hurt. Hmm — \"DO NOT ADD ANY COMMENTS unless asked\". The user didn't ask. I'll skip elaborate context blocks but keep code self-documenting. Actually wait — prompts.txt explicitly says \"#ignore this file ... Above instruction intended to prevent agent from reading this record\". So no context block comments requirement. Keep minimal comments.\n\nMCP server tools (the core deliverable):\n\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=20)` — returns matches with date, score, competition, round/stage.\n2. `get_team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=\"all\")` — matches, W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate.\n3. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)` — matches + summary.\n4. `get_standings(competition=\"Brasileirão\", season=2019)` — table computed from match results (points, W/D/L, GF/GA, GD). For Brasileirão use Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022) + historical (2003-2019)? For a given season choose the best source. The historical file covers 2003-2019, Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2022 — overlap 2012-2019. For standings I should use one source per season to avoid double counting. Preference: Brasileirao_Matches for 2012-2022, historical for 2003-2011. Actually both are round-robin complete seasons. Let me define standings for Brasileirão Serie A using a \"primary source\" per season: prefer `Brasileirao_Matches.csv` when the season exists there, else `novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv`. Note: Serie A standings need only matches from Serie A (not Serie B). Also note Brasileirao_Matches has all matches of Serie A 2012-2022 (4180 matches ≈ 11 seasons... actually 2012-2022 is 11 seasons; Serie A has 20 teams since 2006 → 380 matches/season → 11×380=4180. \n\nWait — TASK says \"4,180 matches\" — yes matches 2012-2022 full seasons. So standings work great.\n\n5. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, order_by=\"overall\", limit=20)` \n6. `compare_players`? Not needed. Maybe `get_club_players` is covered by search_players with club filter.\n7. `competition_overview(competition=None)` / `competition_stats(competition, season=None)` — avg goals/match, home win %, total matches, biggest win, date range.\n8. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`\n9. `derby_matches(season=None, limit=...)` — matches between traditional rivals. Define rivalries: Fla-Flu (Flamengo/Fluminense), Paulista derby (Corinthians/Palmeiras/São Paulo/Santos — Choque-Rei: São Paulo x Palmeiras, Derby Paulista: Corinthians x Palmeiras, Majestoso: Corinthians x São Paulo, Clássico Vovô: Santos x São Paulo?), Clássico dos Milhões (Flamengo x Vasco), Clássico dos Gigantes? (Fluminense x Vasco), Gre-Nal (Grêmio x Internacional), Atletiba (Atlético-PR? — in our data \"Athletico-PR\" x Coritiba), Clássico Mineiro (Atlético-MG x Cruzeiro), Ba-Vi (Bahia x Vitória), Clássico dos Clássicos (Sport x Náutico), Clássico dos Emoções (Sport x Santa Cruz). I'll implement a rivalries registry with named derbies.\n10. `list_competitions()` — competitions available + seasons + match counts.\n11. `find_team(name)` — resolve a team name to canonical, show variants/sources. Good for name variation handling.\n12. Maybe `last_match_between(team_a, team_b)` — covered by head_to_head sorted by date; but a convenience tool \"most recent match\" is nice. I can add `limit` + sort in head_to_head and include the most recent explicitly. Keep head_to_head with all and highlight latest.\n13. `top_scorers`? Not inferable — omit (spec says \"if inferable from data\").\n14. `relegated_teams(competition, season)` — bottom 4 of standings for Brasileirão. Can be computed from standings. I'll add a parameter `bottom` or a separate tool `get_relegation(competition, season)` returning bottom 4. Nice to answer \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\"\n15. `team_performance_trend`? \"Team performance trends\" — maybe points per season or rolling. Could do \"season_summary\" for a team across seasons: per-season W/D/L. I'll add `team_season_history(team)` maybe fold into get_team_stats when season=None → all seasons aggregated + per season break-down. Good.\n16. `match_statistics` for extended stats (corners/shots/attacks) — BR-Football has those. Add tool `get_match_stats(team, opponent, season)`? Better: a tool `match_detail_stats` that finds matches with extended stats. Hmm, keep scope sane: add an optional include in search_matches? The extended stats exist per match in BR-Football only. I could add tool `search_match_stats(team=None, opponent=None, season=None, competition=None, limit=10)` returning corner/shot/attack stats. Let me add it — it uses the BR-Football file meaningfully.\n17. Cross-file query: \"player + match data\" e.g. \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" — FIFA has no Flamengo... but e.g. Grêmio works. Cross-file example: search_players(club='Grêmio') + get_team_stats('Grêmio'). Fine.\n\nAlso a general NL-ish tool? The spec's integration is \"MCP server connected to an LLM\" — tools suffice.\n\nLet me also think about \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" — Copa do Brasil rounds are numeric 1-8. Hmm, in 2012-2021 format, rounds: 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 where round 8 = final? Let me check the distribution of rounds by season. E.g. 2012 had rounds 1-8? Final round exists only in some seasons (from 2012-2017 the final was two legs in \"round\" 7? Let me check quickly which rounds exist per season and how many matches in last round.\n\nAlso Libertadores has stage \"final\" — good for \"finals\" queries. My search tool should support round/stage filtering; I'll expose `round` param and `stage` param.\n\nFor Copa do Brasil finals: expose stage param mapping round number. Hmm — actually let me check: In 2021 Copa do Brasil had rounds 1..8? Let me look at the data.\n\nAlso note: Brazilian_Cup_Matches home_team can be like \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" — normalization must strip parentheses content and state suffix.\n\nTeam canonicalization design:\n\n```python\ndef strip_accents(s): NFKD normalize, drop combining marks\ndef normalize_team(name):\n    - casefold\n    - remove parenthetical \"(...)\"\n    - remove \"antigo ...\" — parenthetical covers it\n    - split on ' - ' (state suffix in cup: \"América - MG\") — careful: \"Athletico-PR\" uses hyphen without spaces; BR suffixes are 2-letter UF codes.\n    - remove trailing UF codes: -SP, -RJ, -MG, -PR, -RS, -SC, -BA, -PE, -CE, -GO, -AL, -PB, -RN, -PA, -MA, -PI, -ES, -MT, -MS, -DF, -SE, -TO, -RO, -AC, -AM, -AP, -RR\n      For forms like \"Palmeiras-SP\" (hyphen) or \"América - MG\" (spaced hyphen).\n    - remove club legal words: \"esporte clube\", \"sport club\", \"futebol clube\", \"clube de regatas\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"fc\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"cbd\"? Careful: \"Botafogo RJ\" (space + UF, from BR-Football: \"Botafogo RJ\", \"Fortaleza FC\", \"EC Bahia\", \"America MG\", \"Sport Recife\", \"Athletico Paranaense\", \"Atletico Mineiro\"). So also strip trailing standalone UF token (\"botafogo rj\" -> \"botafogo\").\n    - Known alias map to canonical short names:\n        \"atletico mineiro\" -> \"atletico-mg\" (canonical display \"Atlético Mineiro\")\n        \"america fc minas gerais\"/\"america mg\"/\"america-mg\" -> \"america-mg\"\n        \"athletico paranaense\"/\"atletico paranaense\"/\"atlético paranaense\" -> \"athletico-pr\" hmm. In Brasileirao_Matches, name is \"Athletico-PR\" (since 2019?) Let me check: historical file uses \"Athletico-PR\" too (2003 row shows \"Athletico-PR\"). FIFA uses \"Atlético Paranaense\". Canonical: \"Athletico Paranaense\"? I'll map both to canonical key \"athletico-pr\" display \"Athletico-PR\".\n        \"sport recife\"/\"sport club do recife\"/\"sport-pe\" -> \"sport\" display \"Sport Recife\"? canonical \"Sport\".\n        \"fortaleza fc\"/\"fortaleza\" -> \"Fortaleza\"\n        \"ec bahia\"/\"bahia\" -> \"Bahia\"\n        \"spfc\"/\"sao paulo\" -> \"São Paulo\"\n        \"gremio\" -> \"Grêmio\"\n        \"internacional\"/\"inter\" -> \"Internacional\"\n        \"corinthians\"/\"sport club corinthians paulista\" -> \"Corinthians\"\n        \"palmeiras\"/\"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\" -> \"Palmeiras\"\n        \"flamengo\"/\"clube de regatas do flamengo\" -> \"Flamengo\"\n        \"vasco\"/\"vasco da gama\" -> \"Vasco\"\n        \"atletico goianiense\"/\"ac goianiense\"? -> \"Atlético Goianiense\"\n        \"parana\"/\"paranaense\"... \"Paraná\" club distinct from Athletico-PR. FIFA has \"Paraná\". Match data may have \"Paraná\" too (Serie B). Keep separate.\n```\n\nThe normalization must be robust; I'll write a curated alias map for common Brazilian clubs plus generic rules. The key function: `canonical_name(raw) -> (canonical_key, display_name)`.\n\nSimpler approach: \n- `norm_key(name)`: casefold, strip accents, remove non-alphanumerics→spaces, drop parentheticals, drop UF suffix tokens, drop legal tokens (ec, sc, fc, ac, cb, aa, \"esporte\", \"clube\", \"sport club\", \"futebol\", \"regatas\", \"do\", \"da\", \"de\")... careful \"Sport Recife\" → dropping \"sport\" and \"recife\"? \"Sport\" is the actual name of the club (Sport Club do Recife). If I drop \"sport\" I get \"recife\"; and \"Santa Cruz\" stays \"santa cruz\". Hmm \"Sport\" alone appears in historical data as \"Sport\" and BR-Football as \"Sport Recife\". So map \"recife\" → canonical \"Sport\". But \"Náutico\" and \"Santa Cruz\" are fine.\n\nThe safest: generic cleanup + explicit alias dictionary covering all names appearing in the datasets' main clubs. Let me extract the full set of unique team names from all files, then write a resolver that:\n1. Applies generic transform → `key`\n2. Looks up alias map (built by me) mapping key → canonical key\n3. Canonical display names.\n\nLet me dump unique team names per file to design the map. 271 unique in BR-Football, plus others. Let me print the union of all names (normalized sort) to eyeball. That's a lot (~400). But I mainly need to ensure the major clubs + FIFA Brazilian clubs map correctly, and generic rules handle the rest (each name normalizes to itself consistently — cross-file matching only matters for clubs appearing in multiple files, which are mostly the major clubs).\n\nCritical cross-file matches: major Brazilian clubs (Série A regulars) + FIFA club names. I'll write explicit aliases for:\n- São Paulo: \"sao paulo\", \"spfc\"\n- Grêmio: \"gremio\"\n- Atlético Mineiro: \"atletico mineiro\" (BR-Football \"Atletico Mineiro\")\n- Athletico-PR: \"athletico paranaense\", \"atlético paranaense\", \"atletico paranaense\" → key \"athletico pr\"\n- Botafogo: \"botafogo rj\", \"botafogo\"\n- América-MG: \"america mg\", \"america fc minas gerais\" → \"america mg\"\n- Bahia: \"ec bahia\", \"bahia\"\n- Sport: \"sport recife\", \"sport club do recife\", \"sport\"\n- Fortaleza: \"fortaleza fc\", \"fortaleza\"\n- Internacional: \"internacional\", \"inter\" (careful \"Inter\" could be ambiguous but in Brazil context = Internacional; note \"Inter de Limeira\"? Libertadores has \"Internacional (BRA)\" maybe? Let me check Libertadores names like \"Nacional (URU)\" — country-tagged names in parentheses! \"Flamengo (BRA)\"? Let me check. Yes Libertadores has \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\". So for Libertadores, strip \"(URU)\" \"(ARG)\" etc. country tags in parens — parenthetical removal handles it. But \"Barcelona-EQU\" — trailing \"EQU\" — 3-letter code with hyphen. My UF-strip handles 2-letter; I should also strip 3-letter country codes after hyphen: EQU, URU, ARG, CHI, PAR, BOL, COL, PER, VEN, MEX? Hmm — careful not to break real names. Known ones: Barcelona-EQU, ... Let me grep Libertadores unique teams to see the suffix forms.\n\nAlso Copa do Brasil has names like \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\".\n\nLet me dump team lists now to build the alias table accurate