 <<'EOF'\nimport csv, re, unicodedata\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef load(path):\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\ndef fold(s):\n    s = unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', s)\n    s = ''.join(c for c in s if not unicodedata.combining(c))\n    return re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9]+', ' ', s.lower()).strip()\n\nUF = {'SP','RJ','MG','PR','RS','SC','BA','PE','CE','GO','AL','PB','RN','PA','MA','PI','ES','MT','MS','DF','SE','TO','RO','AC','AM','AP','RR'}\ndef key(name):\n    s = re.sub(r'\\(.*?\\)', ' ', name)          # parentheticals\n    toks = s.replace('-', ' ').split()\n    toks = [t for t in toks if t.upper() not in UF and len(t) <= 3 and t.isupper() is False or t.lower() in ('spfc',)] # rough\n    # simpler: drop trailing uppercase 2-3 letter tokens\n    out = []\n    for t in s.replace('-', ' ').split():\n        out.append(t)\n    while out and out[-1].isupper() and 2 <= len(out[-1]) <= 3:\n        out.pop()\n    return fold(' '.join(out))\n\nbr = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\nbm = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\n\n# 2019 Serie A in both files?\ns1 = {(r['date'], key(r['home']), key(r['away'])) for r in br if r['tournament']=='Serie A' and r['date'].startswith('2019')}\ns2 = {(r['datetime'][:10], key(r['home_team']), key(r['away_team'])) for r in bm if r['season']=='2019'}\nprint(\"BR-Football 2019 Serie A:\", len(s1), \" Brasileirao file 2019:\", len(s2), \" overlap:\", len(s1 & s2))\ndiff = list(s2 - s1)[:5]\nprint(\"in Brasileirao not in BR-Football:\", diff)\n\n# Athletico bare in Libertadores\nlib = load('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nfor r in lib:\n    if r['home_team'].strip() == 'Athletico' or r['away_team'].strip() == 'Athletico':\n        print(\"Athletico row:\", r['season'], r['home_team'], r['away_team'], r['stage'])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788276404054,"end":1788276404059}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the Copa do Brasil round structure — rounds vary by season; the final is the highest round (8 for 2013-2020, 6 for 2012/2016, 4 for 2021). Hmm wait 2016: rounds 1-7 with 75 in round 1... that's odd but fine. To find finals I'd take max round per season.\n\nRather than hardcoding, for \"finals\" queries on Copa do Brasil I'll compute the max round per season. I can expose a `stage` parameter in search_matches and additionally a convenience: `round` filter. For Copa do Brasil finals, an LLM can first query and see. But to make it easy, maybe a dedicated tool `find_finals(competition, season=None)` that returns the final(s): for Libertadores stage='final'; for Copa do Brasil, max round in that season; for Brasileirão there's no final (league). I'll implement `find_finals` smartly.\n\nAlso interesting: \"Athletico-PR\" in historical/Brasileirao files is sometimes \"Atletico-PR\" (Brasileirao_Matches uses \"Atletico-PR\" while historical uses \"Athletico-PR\"). And Libertadores has both \"Atlético-PR\" and \"Athletico\" (bare). BR-Football has \"Athletico Paranaense\". FIFA has \"Atlético Paranaense\". So alias needed: all → \"athletico-pr\" (canonical display \"Athletico-PR\").\n\nSimilarly \"Atletico-MG\" / \"Atlético-MG\" / \"Atletico Mineiro\" / \"Atlético Mineiro\" → \"Atlético-MG\".\n\"Sao Paulo-SP\" / \"São Paulo\" / \"spfc\" → \"São Paulo\".\n\"Gremio-RS\" / \"Grêmio\" → \"Grêmio\".\n\"America-MG\" / \"América-MG\" / \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" / \"America MG\" → \"América-MG\".\n\"Botafogo-RJ\" / \"Botafogo\" / \"Botafogo RJ\" → \"Botafogo\".\n\"Sport-PE\" / \"Sport\" / \"Sport Recife\" / \"Sport Club do Recife\" → \"Sport\".\n\"Ceara-CE\" / \"Ceará\" / \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → \"Ceará\".\n\"Vitoria-BA\" / \"Vitória\" → \"Vitória\".\n\"Coritiba-PR\" / Coritiba → same.\n\"Parana-PR\" / \"Paraná\" / \"Paraná Clube\"? FIFA \"Paraná\" → \"Paraná\".\n\"Nautico-PE\" / \"Náutico\" → \"Náutico\".\n\"Avai-SC\" / \"Avaí\" → \"Avaí\".\n\"Criciuma-SC\" / \"Criciúma\" → \"Criciúma\".\n\"Goias-GO\" / \"Goiás\" → \"Goiás\".\n\"Atletico-GO\" / \"Atlético-GO\" → \"Atlético-GO\".\n\"Csa-AL\" / \"CSA\" → \"CSA\".\n\"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" / \"Vasco\" → \"Vasco\".\n\"Fortaleza-CE\" / \"Fortaleza\" / \"Fortaleza FC\" → \"Fortaleza\".\n\"EC Bahia\" / \"Bahia-BA\" / \"Bahia\" → \"Bahia\".\n\"Internacional-RS\" / \"Internacional\" → \"Internacional\".\n\"Palmeiras-SP\" / \"Palmeiras\" → \"Palmeiras\".\n\"Chapecoense-SC\" / \"Chapecoense\" → \"Chapecoense\".\n\"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" / \"Red Bull Bragantino\" / \"Bragantino\" → \"Red Bull Bragantino\".\n\"Cuiaba-MT\" / \"Cuiabá\" → \"Cuiabá\" (BR-Football may have \"Cuiaba\"?).\n\"Ponte Preta-SP\" / \"Ponte Preta\" → \"Ponte Preta\".\n\nNon-Brazilian Libertadores teams with suffixes: \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Delfín-EQU\", \"Guaraní (PAR)\", \"Guaraní-PAR\", \"Libertad-PAR\", \"Olimpia-PAR\", \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Nacional-URU\", \"River Plate-URU\", \"Universitario (PER)\", \"Universitario-PER\", \"Trujillanos (VEN)\", \"Nacional (PAR)\". So strip trailing -XXX 2-3 letter tokens and parentheticals. But careful: \"Colo-Colo\" — trailing \"Colo\" is not a code; my rule strips only ALL-CAPS-ish tokens after hyphen? \"Colo-Colo\" → tokens \"colo\",\"colo\" — both lowercase letters. \"Barcelona-EQU\" — \"EQU\" uppercase. Rule: strip trailing token after hyphen if it's uppercase and length 2-3 (UF codes are 2, country codes 2-3). But in files where names are case-normalized (\"Sao Paulo-SP\" — \"SP\" uppercase, good; \"Atletico-MG\" — \"MG\" uppercase, good; \"America-MG\" uppercase MG). In BR-Football: \"Botafogo RJ\" (space, uppercase). \"Athletico Paranaense\" no code. OK rule: remove trailing `-XX` or `-XXX` where the token is uppercase alphabetic (also handle ` - XX` spaced). Also parenthetical removal handles \"(URU)\", \"(BRA)\".\n\nWait, there's a subtlety: \"Athletico\" alone in Libertadores (probably Athletico-PR, but could be... let me check who \"Athletico\" played against — it's from 2021/2022 Libertadores (Athletico-PR). And \"Real Atlético\"?? Interesting — probably \"Real Garcilaso\" renamed... whatever. I'll alias \"athletico\" → \"athletico-pr\"? Risky: \"Athletico\" bare only appears in Libertadores where Brazilian Athletico-PR participated. Hmm, but there's also \"Atlético\" clubs from other countries: \"Atlético Nacional\" (COL), \"Atlético Tucumán\" (ARG), \"Atlético Madrid\" (FIFA). Bare \"Athletico\" in Libertadores 2022 — Athletico-PR played Libertadores 2022 (they were semifinalist). Let me check: which teams did \"Athletico\" play in Libertadores? If opponents are Brazilian teams (e.g., Fortaleza, Flamengo), then yes it's Athletico-PR. Let me verify quickly. Also \"LDU\" (Ecuador), \"Tolima\" (\"Deportes Tolima\" also appears — dedupe not critical for foreign teams).\n\nFor safety, in the alias map I'll map \"athletico\" → Athletico-PR only if data confirms; it's a minor edge case but let me check.\n\nAlso Libertadores \"Rentistas \" has trailing space — strip whitespace.\n\nNow — think about what \"matches\" dataset unification means for stats:\n- `get_team_stats('Palmeiras', season=2023)`: Brasileirao data ends 2022; BR-Football goes to 2023 with Serie A. So 2023 covered by BR-Football.\n- For team stats and h2h, which sources to use? Overlaps: Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022 Serie A) overlaps BR-Football Serie A (2014-2023) and historical (2003-2019). If I naively union all sources, matches get double-counted (same match in two files with slightly different team names). Dedup strategy: a match identity = (date, home_key, away_key). If two sources have the same (date, home, away), treat as one match — merge stats (keep extended stats from BR-Football). Since kickoff times differ across sources (Brasileirao_Matches has time, BR-Football has separate time; historical has only date), I dedupe on exact date+teams. Some sources might differ by a day (rare). This is a reasonable dedup. Actually — do Brasileirao_Matches and BR-Football Serie A have the same dates for the same fixtures? Let me verify with a sample (Palmeiras 2019). Let me check overlap counts.\n\nPlan: unified `Match` list with `sources` field; dedupe by (date, home_key, away_key) keeping first occurrence but preferring richer record (with extended stats) — I'll prefer BR-Football record when available for the extended stats, and record all sources. For standings, restrict to a single source per season (prefer Brasileirao_Matches for Serie A 2012+, historical for 2003-2011, BR-Football Serie B/C when needed...). Hmm, actually with dedup, standings for Brasileirão Serie A 2019 could double count if dates mismatch between files. Let me test dedup overlap: count matches for 2019 Serie A in each file, and how many (date,home,away) collide.\n\nLet me check: Brasileirao 2019: 380 matches. BR-Football Serie A 2019: 380? Historical 2019: 380. If dedup by (date, home, away) works, union 2019 Serie A should be ~380 not 1140.\n\nLet me test quickly. Also check whether BR-Football \"Serie A\" 2014-2016 aligns.\n\nThen competitions naming: canonical competition labels:\n- \"Brasileirão Série A\" ← Brasileirao_Matches + historical (Ano) + BR-Football \"Serie A\"\n- \"Brasileirão Série B\" ← BR-Football \"Serie B\"\n- \"Brasileirão Série C\" ← BR-Football \"Serie C\"\n- \"Copa do Brasil\" ← Brazilian_Cup_Matches + BR-Football \"Copa do Brasil\"\n- \"Copa Libertadores\" ← Libertadores_Matches\n\nFor BR-Football Serie A vs Brasileirao_Matches: same competition — after dedup, competition = \"Brasileirão Série A\".\n\nFor standings source preference: to be safe I'll compute standings from a filtered set of matches by competition+season (deduped). If dedup works correctly, 2019 Serie A = 380 matches → correct standings.\n\nPotential issue: BR-Football Serie A 2014-2016 might have different team name spellings but my normalization handles that. Date mismatch: e.g., same fixture listed with date 2023-09-24 in one file and 2023-09-25 in another? For Serie A 2014-2016 vs Brasileirao_Matches 2014-2016, let me verify overlap empirically.\n\nAlso the OBS column in historical has some notes. Ignore.\n\nThe 'NA' season Libertadores row — handle gracefully (season None).\n\nFIFA data parsing: 18,207 rows, columns as seen. Values like \"€110.5M\", heights \"5'7\", weights \"159lbs\", \"Joined\" \"Jul 1, 2004\". I'll parse: ID, Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, JerseyNumber, Height (keep raw), Weight (raw), Value (parse to numeric M?), Wage. Also skill ratings (Crossing, Finishing, Dribbling etc.) might be useful for \"player attributes\". I'll keep a few skill fields: Crossing, Finishing, ShortPassing, Dribbling, LongPassing, BallControl, ShotPower, Penalties, StandingTackle, GKDiving...? Keep it simple: keep main ones and return on demand. Positions include GK etc. Let me check Position values and the club field for Brazilians.\n\nHeight \"5'7\" and Weight \"159lbs\" — keep raw strings in output (fine for display).\n\nSpecial case: Club can be empty (free agents) — 231 rows or so have NaN club.\n\nNow the MCP server. Using `mcp` SDK 2.1.1 with FastMCP:\n\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nmcp = FastMCP(\"brazilian-soccer\")\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_matches(...) -> str: ...\n```\n\nFastMCP from the mcp package: `from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP`. Run with `mcp.run()` (stdio default).\n\nTools return strings (or dicts — FastMCP can serialize). I'll return structured JSON strings or formatted text? For LLM consumption, formatted markdown text like the examples in the spec is nice, but structured JSON is more programmatically testable. Compromise: return dicts with both a human-readable summary and data rows. The MCP client (Claude) handles JSON well. But the spec shows formatted text answers. I'll return text-formatted summaries (like spec examples) — tests then assert on content. Hmm, for testability and tool use, JSON is safer. I'll return JSON with fields; formatting is the LLM's job. Actually a hybrid: JSON output including \"summary\" strings for common queries. Let me design each tool to return a JSON-serializable dict; FastMCP will convert to structured content. For large lists, cap with limit parameters.\n\nList of tools (final):\n1. `list_competitions()` — competition, seasons, match counts, teams count. No args.\n2. `resolve_team(name)` — canonical name, variants found across datasets, matches count. (Name variation handling demo.)\n3. `search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, round, limit)` \n4. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition, season, limit)` — full record + latest match + per-team wins.\n5. `get_team_stats(team, season, competition, venue)` — W/D/L/GF/GA/win rates + per-competition breakdown when no competition specified? Keep: overall + by competition. And when season omitted → all seasons; include per-season trend table.\n6. `get_standings(competition, season)` — full table with points; champion marked; relegation zone marked (bottom 4).\n7. `find_finals(competition, season)` — finals for Copa do Brasil (max round per season), Libertadores (stage='final').\n8. `search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, max_overall, limit, order)` — FIFA data.\n9. `club_players(club)` — hmm covered by search_players(club=...). Skip.\n10. `competition_stats(competition, season)` — matches, avg goals, home win/draw/away win rates, biggest win, total goals, date range.\n11. `biggest_wins(competition, season, limit)` — sorted by goal difference.\n12. `derby_matches(derby, season, limit)` — named rivalries registry; list derbies when no derby given? Or `list_derbies()`. I'll make derby_matches with optional derby name; if None, return all derby matches for season (any rivalry pair).\n13. `match_stats(team, opponent, season, competition, limit)` — extended stats (corners/shots/attacks) from BR-Football.\n14. `relegated_teams(competition, season)` — bottom-4 of standings. (Or fold