multiple times — e.g., Copa do Brasil two legs (both directions) and possibly same pairing twice in same direction? Rare. I'll join on (competition, season, home_key, away_key) and if multiple candidates, pick nearest date. Good enough for extended stats.\n\nLet me verify counts per competition/season across sources, then finalize.\n\n2. **\"Athletico\" bare in Libertadores** = Athletico-PR (played Boca Juniors 2019 R16 — Athletico-PR did play Boca in 2019 Libertadores round of 16! Yes. And 2022 semifinal vs Palmeiras — Athletico-PR reached 2022 Libertadores final (they lost to Flamengo in the final — the \"NA\" season row: Flamengo x Athletico final, which is the 2022 final played 2022-10-29... wait the NA season final row — Flamengo beat Athletico-PR 1-0 in the 2022 final. But the row has season \"NA\" — it's the 2022 final misfiled? Actually the 2022 Libertadores final was Flamengo 1-0 Athletico Paranaense on Oct 29, 2022 (in Guayaquil). The NA row is probably that match with missing season. I'll keep NA season as None and treat gracefully. Could even backfill: if season is NA, try to infer from date? I'll just leave it as unknown season but still searchable by teams/date.)\n\nSo alias: bare \"athletico\" → Athletico-PR. It's used only in Libertadores for the Brazilian club. Fine — alias map: \"athletico\" → \"athletico-pr\" display \"Athletico-PR\". Hmm wait, but that would also make an alias for e.g. someone querying \"athletico\". Good.\n\nAlso \"Athletico-PR\" spelled \"Atletico-PR\" in Brasileirao_Matches and \"Atlético-PR\" in Libertadores — all → \"athletico-pr\".\n\nOne more check on FIFA club names: \"Atlético Paranaense\" → atlético paranaense → key \"atletico paranaense\" → alias → athletico-pr. \"Atlético Mineiro\" → \"atletico mineiro\" → alias \"atletico-mg\". \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → paren strip → \"america fc\" → \"america fc\" drop legal token \"fc\" → \"america\" → alias → \"america-mg\"? Careful — \"América\" could also be América-RN, América-SC... but in FIFA context it's América-MG. Alias \"america\" → \"america-mg\" is risky if another \"América\" exists in match data... yes! Historical has \"América-RN\" (key \"america\") and \"América-MG\" (key \"america mg\"). Uh oh: \"América-RN\" and \"América-MG\" both strip UF → both become \"america\"! Collision!\n\nSo the UF-stripping creates ambiguity when two clubs share a base name with different states. In Brazil this is common (América-MG/RN/SC, Atlético-GO/MG/PR, Botafogo-RJ/PB/SP...). But within Serie A datasets, which collisions actually occur?\n- \"América-MG\" vs \"América-RN\": both appear in historical (América-RN 19 matches in 2007? and América-MG 57). So \"america\" is ambiguous in historical data!\n- \"Atlético-GO\" vs \"Atlético-MG\": both appear (\"atletico go\" vs \"atletico\" — wait \"Atlético-GO\" strips GO → \"atletico\", and \"Atlético-MG\" strips MG → \"atletico\". COLLISION! Historical has both Atlético-MG (314) and Atlético-GO (76). \n\nThat's a real problem: my quick dedup test above showed key 'atletico' for atletico-mg AND atletico-go rows (\"2019-06-02 atletico csa\" — that's Atlético-MG; \"Atletico-GO\" would also become 'atletico'). This would corrupt stats!\n\nSolution: **do NOT blindly strip UF suffixes**. Instead, keep the UF as part of the key when present, and use an alias map for known mappings. I.e.:\n- \"Atletico-MG\" → key \"atletico mg\"\n- \"Atletico-GO\" → key \"atletico go\"\n- \"Atletico Mineiro\" (BR-Football) → \"atletico mg\" via alias\n- \"Atlético-MG\" → \"atletico mg\"\n- \"América-MG\" → \"america mg\"; \"América-RN\" → \"america rn\"; \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → \"america\" → alias \"america mg\" (since FIFA's América is América-MG)\n- \"Botafogo-RJ\" → \"botafogo rj\"; \"Botafogo RJ\" (BR-Football) → \"botafogo rj\"; \"Botafogo\" (FIFA, Libertadores) → alias \"botafogo\" → \"botafogo rj\" (Botafogo-PB rarely relevant)\n- \"São Paulo-SP\" → \"sao paulo sp\"?? Hmm but then \"São Paulo\" (Libertadores) → \"sao paulo\" — alias needed to unify. This is the crux: some files include UF suffix, others don't.\n\nBetter design: **canonical registry keyed by club base name + disambiguation**:\n\nFor each raw name, compute:\n- parenthetical removal\n- accent folding, lowercase, punctuation → spaces\n- detect trailing UF token (2-letter, in UF set) → (base, uf)\n- base key with legal tokens stripped (\"ec\", \"sc\", \"fc\", \"ac\", \"cb\", \"aa\", \"clube\", \"esporte\", \"futebol\", \"regatas\", \"do\", \"da\", \"de\", \"sport club\", \"sporting club\"?) — careful \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → \"ceara sporting club\" → strip \"sporting club\" → \"ceara\" → alias \"ceara\" → \"ceara ce\"? But \"Ceara-CE\" → base \"ceara\" uf CE. And bare \"Ceará\" → \"ceara\". Unify: if only one club with base \"ceara\" exists in registry, bare name maps to it. \n\nTwo-tier resolution:\n1. Build registry of ALL distinct (base, uf) from match data where uf is present.\n2. For names WITHOUT uf: if base maps to exactly one registered club → that club. If ambiguous → keep as its own distinct identity UNLESS an explicit alias resolves it (e.g. FIFA \"América\" → América-MG; FIFA \"Atlético Paranaense\" → Athletico-PR; FIFA \"Botafogo\" → Botafogo-RJ; \"Atlético Mineiro\" → Atlético-MG; \"Sport Recife\" → Sport-PE; \"Internacional\" unambiguous; \"Vasco da gama\" → Vasco).\n\nHmm, wait. Simpler alternative: **make the canonical key = base name (no UF), and only disambiguate true collisions with explicit aliases**. The true collision bases in our data: let me enumerate bases with multiple UFs across all files. Then for those specific bases, the key includes UF or explicit alias. For everything else, base alone is the canonical key (nice: \"São Paulo-SP\" and \"São Paulo\" both → \"sao paulo\"; \"Atletico-MG\"→\"atletico mg\"... no wait, base of \"Atletico-MG\" is \"atletico\" with uf MG).\n\nUgh. Let me think again about which bases are ambiguous:\n- \"atletico\": Atlético-MG, Atlético-GO, (Atlético-PR appears as \"Atletico-PR\"/\"Atlético-PR\"/\"Athletico-PR\" — base \"atletico pr\"? No: \"Atletico-PR\" → UF PR → base \"atletico\", uf PR). So base \"atletico\" has MG, GO, PR → ambiguous 3 ways!\n- \"america\": MG, RN (and SC not in data?)\n- \"botafogo\": RJ (and PB? BR-Football Serie C has \"Botafogo PB\"! Let me check — likely yes since Serie C 2014-2023 includes Botafogo-PB.)\n- \"vasco\": RJ only (base \"vasco da gama\" vs \"vasco\")\n- \"sport\": PE only\n- \"internacional\": RS only\n- \"nacional\" — foreign (URU, PAR) — but those come with country codes mostly stripped as parentheticals; \"Nacional-URU\" → uf-strip? \"URU\" isn't a Brazilian UF but my earlier rule stripped any 2-3 uppercase. With UF-set-only stripping, \"Nacional-URU\" → base \"nacional uru\"? Hmm. For foreign teams I should strip country codes too. Foreign teams with suffix: \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Delfín-EQU\", \"Guaraní-PAR\", \"Libertad-PAR\", \"Olimpia-PAR\", \"Nacional-URU\", \"Nacional-PAR\"(as \"Nacional (PAR)\"), \"River Plate-URU\", \"Universitario-PER\". The UF set is exactly Brazilian states; I'll add a small country-code set {URU, ARG, EQU, PAR, PER, VEN, COL, CHI, BOL, MEX} for trailing hyphenated tokens. But careful \"Guaraní (PAR)\" paren already removed. OK.\n\nSo design: \n```\ndef split_name(raw) -> (base, suffix):\n    remove parentheticals; \n    replace hyphens with spaces EXCEPT keep tokens;\n    tokens; if last token is 2-letter UF or 3-letter country code AND len(tokens)>1: suffix=token, drop it\n```\nBut \"Colo-Colo\" → tokens [\"colo\",\"colo\"], last not code, fine. \"América-MG\" → base \"america\", suffix MG. \"Atletico-MG\" → \"atletico\"/MG.\n\nThen canonical resolution:\n```\nalias_by_base_uf = {(\"atletico\",\"MG\"): \"atletico-mg\", ...}\n```\nHmm, I think cleanest implementation:\n\n**Canonical club key** = base if unambiguous else \"base-uf\". I'll hardcode the disambiguation map for known ambiguous bases:\n\nAMBIGUOUS = {\n  \"atletico\": {\"MG\": \