ost frequent raw variant). Implementation: data.py tracks raw variant counts per key; display = DISPLAY.get(key) or most-common raw (title-cased as-is).\n\n**Competition normalization**:\n\n```python\nCOMPETITIONS = {\n  'Brasileirão Série A', 'Brasileirão Série B', 'Brasileirão Série C', 'Copa do Brasil', 'Copa Libertadores'\n}\ndef competition_key(q): fold → 'serie a'/'serie b'/'serie c'/'copa do brasil'/'libertadores'/'brasileirao'→serie a default etc.\n```\n\n**Match model:**\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Match:\n    competition: str        # canonical label\n    season: int | None\n    date: date | None\n    time: str | None\n    stage: str | None       # Libertadores stage / cup round label\n    round_number: int | None\n    home: str               # canonical key\n    away: str\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    source: str             # file short name\n    venue: str | None       # stadium (historical only)\n    has_stats: bool / stats: MatchStats | None\n```\n\nMatchStats: corners home/away, shots, attacks, ht scores.\n\n**data.py (SoccerData)**:\n- loads players (fifa)\n- loads all match sources into raw records, then builds the primary unified match list per policy + all_matches list (each with source) — actually simpler: build `self.matches` = primary unified list. For each (competition, season): pick source by priority:\n  - Série A: order [brasileirao_matches, historico, brfootball_serie_a] — first with ≥1 match for that season.\n  - Série B/C: [brfootball]\n  - Copa do Brasil: [cup_file, brfootball] per season? Cup file has 2012-2021, more matches; 2022-2023 only brf. Order [cup_file, brfootball] — but wait 2021: cup=108, brf=111. brf has MORE for 2021. Hmm. Cup file 2021 is truncated at round 4 (108) but includes rounds properly; brf 111. Honestly either is fine. Policy \"first source with matches\" → cup file wins 2021. It's the dedicated cup file with round structure. I'll keep [cup_file, brfootball] — but then 2021 finals? Cup file 2021 has no rounds beyond 4 — the 2021 final not in cup file, and brf has 111 matches for 2021 possibly including the final. Hmm hmm. For find_finals(2021): max round in cup file = 4 → wrong \"final\". \n\n    Alternative policy for Copa do Brasil: per season, prefer the source with MORE matches (brf 2021: 111 wins; other seasons cup file wins). Then 2021 uses brf (111 matches, no round info — finals not identifiable). Cup file 2021: rounds 1-4 only (the file is truncated). So with either policy 2021 finals aren't properly identifiable. What does brf 2021 Copa do Brasil contain — maybe only from a certain point? 111 matches... The real 2021 Copa do Brasil had 79 matches total (from round of 32?). Hmm whatever. With \"more matches wins\" policy: 2021→brf(111) no round data → find_finals returns nothing for 2021 with a note. With cup-first policy: max round 4 (semifinal-ish, actually 2021 format: round 3 = round of 32? The 2021 cup had rounds: 1 (first), 2 (second), 3 (third), then QF, SF, final...) — mislabeled as \"final\".\n\n    Decision: cup-first policy for all cup-file seasons (2012-2021). find_finals for Copa do Brasil = max round per season; for 2021 the data is truncated (documented limitation). Add note in find_finals when a season's max-round < 5. Good enough — data is data.\n\n  - Libertadores: [libertadores]\n  \n  Wait — actually, hmm, for Copa do Brasil, should I use BOTH cup file and brf when they have different matches?? 2021 brf has 111 vs cup 108 — are they disjoint sets? The overlap-by-date was 0 (dates differ). Different match sets entirely! Ugh. The truth: 2021 Copa do Brasil had 74? matches... Let me check how many DISTINCT (season, normalized home, normalized away) each has. If they're the same fixtures with different dates, cup-first is right. If brf has fixtures the cup file lacks (later rounds), a union deduped by (season, home, away) might be better for the cup. Let me check 2021: distinct fixture pairs in each.\n\nActually — I realize I should stop over-analyzing the cup edge case. Practical approach: for Copa do Brasil use cup file for 2012-2021 (it has rounds — richer), brf for 2022-2023. That's it. The 2021 truncation is a documented data limitation.\n\nLet me quickly check the fixture-set overlap for 2019 (cup=120, brf=117) to confirm they're the same fixtures with different dates — if so, cup-first is clearly correct.\n\n**Standings**: compute from primary matches of Série A for a season. Verify 2019: Flamengo champion with 90 pts. I'll validate in tests.\n\n**Relegation**: bottom 4 by points in Série A standings. 2020 relegated: Coritiba, Vasco, Goiás, Sport (in reality). Validate in test.\n\n**Derbies** (rivalries registry):\n```python\nDERBIES = {\n  'Fla-Flu': ('flamengo', 'fluminense'),\n  'Clássico dos Milhões': ('flamengo', 'vasco'),\n  'Gre-Nal': ('gremio', 'internacional'),\n  'Clássico Mineiro': ('atletico-mg', 'cruzeiro'),\n  'Derby Paulista': ('corinthians', 'palmeiras'),\n  'Majestoso': ('corinthians', 'sao paulo'),\n  'Choque-Rei': ('palmeiras', 'sao paulo'),\n  'Clássico Alvinegro? no... 'San-São': ('santos', 'sao paulo'),\n  'Ba-Vi': ('bahia', 'vitoria'),\n  'Clássico dos Clássicos': ('sport', 'nautico'),\n  'Clássico dos Emoções': ('sport', 'santa cruz'),\n  'Atletiba': ('athletico-pr', 'coritiba'),\n  'Clássico Vovô': ('santos', 'corinthians')? — actually Clássico Vovô = Santos x Corinthians? No — \"Clássico Vovô\" is Santos x Corinthians? Let me think... Clássico Vovô = Santos vs Corinthians (oldest derby in Brazil? Actually the \"Vovô\" derby is Santos x Corinthians? Hmm — I believe Clássico Vovô is Santos x Corinthians... no wait. The oldest derby in Brazil is \"Clássico Vovô\" — Santos x Corinthians? Let me recall: \"Clássico Vovô\" is the nickname for Santos vs Corinthians (played since 1913?)... Actually I'm fairly sure \"Clássico Vovô\" refers to Santos x Corinthians — the \"grandfather derby\". Hmm, some sources say Clássico Vovô = Santos x Corinthians (derby between the two oldest clubs in São Paulo state? Portuguesa?...) To be safe I'll use well-known ones only:\n  - Fla-Flu: Flamengo x Fluminense ✓\n  - Clássico dos Milhões: Flamengo x Vasco ✓\n  - Gre-Nal: Grêmio x Internacional ✓\n  - Clássico Mineiro: Atlético-MG x Cruzeiro ✓ (a.k.a. \"Atlético-MG x Cruzeiro\")\n  - Derby Paulista: Corinthians x Palmeiras ✓\n  - Majestoso: Corinthians x São Paulo ✓\n  - Choque-Rei: Palmeiras x São Paulo ✓ (also known as \"Derby de São Paulo\"? no, Choque-Rei is Palmeiras x São Paulo ✓)\n  - San-São: Santos x São Paulo ✓\n  - Clássico da Saudade? no.\n  - Ba-Vi: Bahia x Vitória ✓\n  - Atletiba: Athletico-PR x Coritiba ✓\n  - Clássico dos Clássicos: Sport x Náutico ✓\n  - Clássico dos Emoções: Sport x Santa Cruz ✓\n  - Come-Fogo? no. \n  - Clássico Rei do Ceará (Ceará x Fortaleza)? ✓ \"Clássico-Rei\": Ceará x Fortaleza — add it.\n  - Clássico Verde-Vermelho? skip.\n}\n```\nThat's a nice registry of 13 named derbies, all with teams in the data. ✓\n\n**MCP tools final list** (FastMCP):\n1. `list_competitions()` → competitions with seasons, match counts, team counts.\n2. `resolve_team(name)` → canonical key, display, variants (raw names + sources + counts), total matches.\n3. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, round=None, limit=25)` → matches list (date, teams, score, competition, round/stage) + total_count + truncated flag.\n4. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None, limit=None)` → played matches + wins/draws/losses per side + goals + latest match.\n5. `get_team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all')` → record; when season None → aggregate + per-season; per-competition breakdown included.\n6. `get_standings(competition='Série A', season)` → table with pos, team, P, W, D, L, GF, GA, GD, Pts; champion flag; relegated (bottom 4) marked.\n7. `get_relegation(competition, season)` → bottom 4. (Hmm — I said maybe fold into standings. I'll keep both; get_relegation is cheap and directly answers a spec question.)\n8. `find_finals(competition, season=None)` → final matches for Libertadores (stage=final) / Copa do Brasil (max round per season) / note for leagues.\n9. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, min_potential=None, limit=20, sort='overall')`.\n10. `get_club_players(club, position=None, limit=None)` — hmm redundant with search_players(club=...). Skip. Keep search_players only. Actually a \"club profile\" joining player data + match stats could be a nice cross-file query: `get_club_overview(club)` → team stats summary + squad from FIFA if present. That demonstrates cross-file queries. Add `get_club_overview(club)`.\n11. `competition_stats(competition, season=None)` → matches, goals, avg goals/match, home/away win %, draw %, biggest win, top scoring teams? keep: matches count, avg goals, home win rate, biggest win.\n12. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` → largest margins.\n13. `derby_matches(derby=None, season=None, competition=None, limit=50)` → derby registry info + matches between rivals.\n14. `search_match_stats(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, limit=20)` → extended stats from BR-Football (corners, shots, attacks, HT result).\n\nThat's 13 tools. Good coverage of the spec's categories:\n- Match queries: 3 ✓\n- Team queries: 5, 10 ✓\n- Player queries: 9 ✓\n- Competition: 6, 7, 8, 1 ✓\n- Statistical: 11, 12, 14 ✓\n- Derbies: 13 ✓\n- Name variations: 2 ✓\n\n**Natural language**: The spec says the LLM processes queries via tools. I'll also accept fuzzy team names in all tools (resolve_team internally, error with suggestions if not found — suggest closest matches via difflib).\n\nTeam resolution for user input: `resolve(name)` → key; if unknown, difflib.get_close_matches against known keys and display names → raise ValueError with suggestions; the MCP tool catches and returns helpful message. \n\n**Output formats**: dicts (JSON-serializable). FastMCP will return structured content + text fallback. Keep numbers rounded appropriately.\n\n**Performance**: load once at module import (server startup). ~40k rows total CSV — fast (<2s). Player queries on 18k rows — linear scans fine. Match queries on ~14k primary matches — fine.\n\nWait — I should double check the fixture overlap for Copa do Brasil (are cup and brf the same fixtures different dates?). And check \"Botafogo PB\" presence in BR-Football, plus build the full collision report after implementing normalize. Let me now write the code, then run a collision report script, then tests.\n\nAlso — one more check — BR-Football \"Athletico Paranaense\" vs \"Atletico Paranaense\" variants? and \"Ceara\" without accent? Earlier sample: 'Athletico Paranaense', 'Fortaleza FC', 'EC Bahia', 'Botafogo RJ', 'Sport Recife', 'America MG', 'Atletico Mineiro'. There may be more variants deeper (Serie C teams: \"Botafogo PB\"?). I'll run the report after coding.\n\n**MCP testing**: check mcp 2.1.1 client API. `from mcp import Client` and transports: `mcp.client.stdio.stdio_client(ServerParameters(...))`. In mcp 2.x, ClientSession: `from mcp.client.session import ClientSession`. Usage:\n\n```python\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nserver_params = StdioServerParameters(command=sys.executable, args=[\"server.py\"])\nasync with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n    async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n        await session.initialize()\n        tools = await session.list_tools()\n        result = await session.call_tool(\"search_matches\", {\"team\": \"Flamengo\", \"opponent\": \"Fluminense\", \"limit\": 5})\n```\n\nIn mcp 2.1.1, is `ClientSession` exported from `mcp` top-level? Possibly `from mcp import Client`. Let me check the installed package. Also FastMCP location: `from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP`. Let me verify quickly. Also check `mcp.shared_memory` for in-memory testing.\n\nLet me check the mcp package API now, then write all the code.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d97bcf4001WZvCJ8tr7ZJJGK","sessionID":"ses_fa26ff80dffeDQKLW66czjBP2c","messageID":"msg_05d9694de001yir0EBItcBhSqA"}}
