ture `soccer`\n- `tests/test_normalize.py` — team name variations, dates, money\n- `tests/test_match_queries.py`\n- `tests/test_team_queries.py`\n- `tests/test_player_queries.py`\n- `tests/test_competition_queries.py`\n- `tests/test_statistics.py`\n- `tests/test_mcp_server.py` — end-to-end via stdio transport + Client\n\nBDD GWT structure: each test has a docstring naming the Feature/Scenario, and Given/When/Then comments.\n\nFacts to assert (verified):\n- 2019 Serie A: Flamengo champion 90 pts; top3 Flamengo 90, Palmeiras 74, Santos 74; relegated [Cruzeiro, Chapecoense, CSA, Avaí] (bottom4 order: rows[-4:] = positions 17-20). Let me double-check the order printed: ['Cruzeiro', 'Chapecoense', 'CSA', 'Avaí'].\n- 2020 Serie A: champion Flamengo; relegated [Vasco da Gama, Goiás, Coritiba, Botafogo].\n- 2019 Palmeiras row: 74 pts, 21W 11D 6L, 61GF 32GA.\n- Fla-Flu h2h: 44 matches, Flamengo 18 wins, Fluminense 14, draws 12; latest 2023-11-11? Hmm — \"latest_match date 2023-11-11\" — that's from... wait Flamengo x Fluminense 2023-11-11? That's Brasileirão 2023 (BR-Football primary for 2023) — Flamengo 3-0 Fluminense? OK.\n- Libertadores 2018 final: two legs, aggregate winner River Plate.\n- Copa do Brasil 2020 final: Palmeiras.\n- Corinthians 2022 home Serie A: 15 recorded (data_note about unplayed); fixtures=19 total in search.\n- Corinthians 2019 home: let me compute for the spec example (2019 data is complete). Actually spec example says 2022 — I'll test both: 2019 home record (complete season) + 2022 partial with note. Let me get 2019 Corinthians home numbers.\n- biggest Libertadores win: River Plate 8-0 Jorge Wilstermann (2017).\n- competition_stats Serie A: 8321 matches? That's primary Série A total (2003-2023): 8403? Earlier competitions_overview printed Série A 8403 total (matches incl. unplayed), competition_stats counts played=8321. avg 2.57, home 49.7%.\n- Neymar Jr overall 92, club Paris Saint-Germain; Casemiro 88; Alisson 85.\n- Brazilian players >=88: Neymar 92, Casemiro 88, Coutinho 88, Marcelo 88, Thiago Silva 88... (first 6: Neymar Jr, Casemiro, Coutinho, Marcelo, Thiago Silva, +1 more).\n- Gabriel Barbosa: not in dataset; similar returns Gabriel Jesus etc.\n- search_match_stats Palmeiras 2023: total 38? earlier: Flamengo 2023 Serie A stats 38 matches. Palmeiras sample has corners 3-4 etc.\n- Grêmio club_overview: squad 20 players, comps include Série B (relegated 2021? Grêmio played Série B 2022 ✓).\n- resolve_team('Corinthians') → variants incl 'Corinthians-SP', 'Corinthians'.\n- derby Gre-Nal 2019: 2 matches.\n\nFor the MCP end-to-end test with the v2 Client over stdio:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, sys\nfrom mcp import Client, StdioServerParameters\n\nasync def main():\n    params = StdioServerParameters(command=sys.executable, args=[\"server.py\"], cwd=repo_root)\n    async with Client(params) as client:\n        tools = await client.list_tools()\n        result = await client.call_tool(\"get_standings\", {\"competition\": \"Brasileirão Série A\", \"season\": 2019})\n        # result.structured_content or result.content\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nClientSession.call_tool returns CallToolResult with `.content` (list of content blocks) and possibly `.structured_content`. Since our tools return dicts, MCPServer converts to structured content + text JSON fallback. Let me verify how the result looks — quick manual run before writing the test. Also `list_tools()` — Client has list_tools? The ClientSession has list_tools; the new Client wraps ClientSession. Check quickly.\n\nOne concern: `Client(StdioServerParameters(command=..., args=[...]))` — does Client accept cwd? StdioServerParameters has fields: command, args, env, cwd? Let me check. In mcp 2.x StdioServerParameters is a pydantic model with command, args, env, cwd probably. I'll pass cwd=repo root; or rely on server.py resolving paths via `Path(__file__).parent.parent` — KAGGLE_DIR is derived from package location (`brazilian_soccer/../data/kaggle`) — absolute