 m.played or m.home_goals is None or m.away_goals is None:\n        continue\n```\nThat narrows afterwards? After `continue` guard, mypy narrows m.home_goals to int? Attributes of a frozen dataclass slot class — mypy does narrow attribute access after a None check within the same scope, as long as no intervening calls. Should work.\n\nThere are many occurrences (186-188, 746-755, etc.) — lines in head_to_head goals, standings table, competition_stats, biggest_wins. These all follow `if m.played` or filtered pools. Options: fix each with explicit None-guards, or... simpler: since goals are Optional by model but always set when played, I could make the guard explicit in the loops.\n\nRather than chase ~50 narrow errors one by one, a pragmatic approach: fix the model properties (self-contained), the normalize no-redef, and add explicit guards at the hot spots. But 47 errors in queries.py — most are the same Optional-arithmetic pattern repeated. Let me look at where they cluster: 186-188 (head_to_head loop), 746-755 (biggest_wins sort/keys), plus standings/competition_stats areas.\n\nHonestly the cleanest: introduce a helper on Match:\n```python\ndef goal_pair(self) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n    if self.home_goals is None or self.away_goals is None:\n        return None\n    return self.home_goals, self.away_goals\n```\nand use it everywhere with `pair = m.goal_pair(); if pair is None: continue; gf, ga = pair`. That's a lot of refactoring...\n\nAlternative: run mypy with narrower scope? The task says \"make sure the code builds and tests pass\" — mypy strictness wasn't requested. The lint (ruff) passes; tests pass. Full mypy strictness on Optional arithmetic is nice-to-have. But since I started, let me at least fix the genuinely typed issues properly — it improves code quality. The Optional-goal arithmetic is guarded by runtime checks (played property), so it's not a real bug class.\n\nLet me do targeted fixes:\n1. models.py result property + payload annotation.\n2. normalize.py redef.\n3. queries.py: use a small local pattern — in each aggregation loop, unpack via goal_pair helper. Let me count the actual spots by looking at the code... Actually, an easier global approach for queries.py: in `_team_record` (the main one), standings loop, head_to_head loop, competition_stats, biggest_wins — each starts with a played filter. I'll add the explicit `m.home_goals is None or m.away_goals is None` to the filters so mypy narrows.\n\nLet me enumerate mypy errors precisely and fix them.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dc3880700134nYEAoZ5U57lj","sessionID":"ses_fa26ff80dffeDQKLW66czjBP2c","messageID":"msg_05dc353a4001dSKzsZ7EvVNgHS"}}
