{"type":"text","timestamp":1788279754417,"sessionID":"ses_fa26ff80dffeDQKLW66czjBP2c","part":{"id":"prt_05dc78388001E8NHFJ24Ycqndn","messageID":"msg_05dc76b10001KMnTkBH2bT690z","sessionID":"ses_fa26ff80dffeDQKLW66czjBP2c","type":"text","text":"Complete. Implemented the Brazilian Soccer MCP server per TASK.md:\n\n**Code** (all in the working directory)\n- `server.py` — MCP server (mcp 2.x `MCPServer`, stdio) exposing **15 tools** covering all five required query categories: match search/head-to-head, team stats, player search, standings/finals/relegation, and statistical analysis (derbies, biggest wins, corners/shots/attacks)\n- `brazilian_soccer/` — `normalize.py` (canonicalizes `Palmeiras-SP` / `Palmeiras` / `Sociedade Esportiva Palmeiras` / FIFA spellings to one club, keeps state-sharing names distinct, handles Libertadores country tags), `dates.py` (ISO + Brazilian date formats, NA markers), `models.py`, `data.py` (loads all 6 CSVs, deduplicates overlapping files via a primary-source policy so aggregates never double count, joins extended stats), `queries.py`\n\n**Verification**\n- 90 BDD Given/When/Then pytest scenarios pass (2.9s), including end-to-end tests over the real stdio MCP transport; 97% package coverage\n- ruff and mypy both clean\n- Results validated against real-world facts: 2019 champion Flamengo (90 pts), 2020 relegated quartet, 2018 Libertadores final Boca–River; data gaps (e.g. unrecorded 2022 late-season scores) are disclosed via `data_note` fields rather than silently wrong numbers\n\n**Docs** — `README.md` rewritten with quick start, tool catalog, data-handling notes, and license attributions.","time":{"start":1788279751560,"end":1788279754415}}}
